#: processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn:159
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Bean `16ei`, arc `3fva`. After Task_RepositoryGates, WHATEVER it concluded (`needs:` its two corpus parts, with `always()`), and after Task_QaWorkingCopy, whose copy it restores and publishes rather than building a second time (bean `jtfk`), `qa:publish --github` writes this run's `test/results` trees to the orphan `qa-reports` branch: `main/<sha>/` on a push to main, `pr/<n>/<head-sha>/` on a same-repository PR (owner rulings D1, D3). A fork PR's token is read-only, so it is skipped with a `::notice` naming why. It does NOT reach GW_Join: a red publish is its own red and never decides mergeability, and the hard gates never wait on it. The only job here holding `contents: write`, which is also how `bun run cat gates` knows not to run it locally. A CALL ACTIVITY: the key derivation, the fork skip and the never-`-f` retry loop are `qa-publish.bpmn`, which also draws the retention run that takes entries off the branch again."
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn:74
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Bean `jtfk`, issue #2456. `bun run qa:working-copy` (bean `72a8`) — the bootstrap kg-export, then every declared QA writer — used to run INSIDE each job that reads the QA tree, and `qa-publish` ran the same writers again: nine builds of one tree from one commit, measured at 2,284 of 4,714 runner-seconds (48 %) on run 37659324493. Now the `qa-working-copy` job builds it once and uploads everything the build changed as an artifact with a sha256 manifest; each reader downloads it, checks the manifest against the hash this job reported and every restored file against the manifest, and only then judges. An artifact rather than a cache, because a cache key built from less than the real inputs serves stale data; an artifact is per run and exact. It judges nothing, so it is not a gate and does not reach GW_Join; a failed build fails every reader at its first step (they run `if: !cancelled()`), never a skip. Drawn feeding GW_Readers, the four tasks that wait for it; within them `typescript-static` and `gates-standalone` read no QA tree and do not wait, and `qa-publish` waits for it as well as for Task_RepositoryGates."
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn:60
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Bean `qbco`, issue #2456 (owner, 2026-10-07: \"1y, 2y, 3y, 4y\"). On a PUSH to `main` only; on any other event it is skipped and nothing waits for it. A merge-button merge of an up-to-date PR carries a tree CI has already tested. `verdict-reuse.sh` looks up the runs of this workflow on the merged head (the merge commit's second parent) and answers `reuse=true` only when one concluded `success`, ran every job a reuse would skip, and tested exactly this tree: for a `pull_request` run, the `tested-tree:` its Task_QaWorkingCopy job wrote into its check run's summary; for a dispatch, the head's own tree. Then the four `bun test` shards (part of Task_TypeScript), the three e2e shards (Task_E2E) and the standalone ratchet (part of Task_RepositoryGates) are skipped on this push, and the summary names the run whose verdict stands. Everything that judges the tree against `main` or reads `qa-reports` still runs. A squash, an API error, a missing record or a mismatch is `reuse=false` and every job runs: could-not-determine is never green. Drawn before the fork because the jobs it can skip wait for it; the others do not. Since the same ruling, a newer push to `main` also cancels an older main run still in progress (the workflow's `concurrency`), so main's verdict history is thinned by decision."
msgstr ""

