#: processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn:105
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Install Chromium, check the rendered BPMN SVGs are current, then run the Playwright suite, which includes the accessibility checks. Hard: a failure blocks the PR. SHARDED since bean `dlqu` (drawn as one task, deliberately: a multi-instance marker would add a BPMN term to every instance's UML overview, about 75 generated files, for a picture this sentence already gives): the suite runs as three `e2e-shard` jobs (`E2E_SHARD=i/3`, one Playwright worker per core in each), and each shard is its own required check, so a shard that failed, was cancelled or never ran is a red check of its own. Until bean `5p4m` (issue #2456) an `e2e` job carried one check name for the three and completed the task only when every instance succeeded; it was ~1 s of work behind a runner request that waited minutes, and was removed. Measured before the split: `playwright test` alone was 4m33s of a 5m16s job, the workflow's slowest once `bun test` ran in parallel. The BPMN-render and `.bat`-wrapper checks run in shard 1 only, so they are asked once."
msgstr ""

#: processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn:35
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Joins every job before asking anything, then GW_Hard asks specifically about the HARD gates — the two warn-only results reach the same join but never the gate, so this lane's merge/block call is deliberately blind to a red warn-only step it also ran. No count is given here on purpose: this paragraph carried \"seven jobs, five hard\" and then \"six-and-four\", and each was wrong the next time a job was split or folded (beans `om30`, `7fu5`). Count the `# bpmn-node:` lines in the workflow instead."
msgstr ""

